WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.086
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 08 of the

2
00:00:04.086 --> 00:00:08.525
Federal Modernization
Workbook: DNS, DHCP and IPAM.

3
00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:13.089
It's for the network and DDI engineers
who will design the resolution paths,

4
00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:16.475
move authority off Active
Directory, and run the result.

5
00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:19.625
It comes in two parts of about
twenty-five minutes each,

6
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:22.075
and four modules you
can watch on their own.

7
00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:24.187
Part 1 covers the hard surfaces:

8
00:00:24.187 --> 00:00:28.329
split-brain DNS and what Active
Directory DNS really carries,

9
00:00:28.329 --> 00:00:32.146
then the places queries escape
agency policy, at the edge,

10
00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:34.746
at the endpoint and on the access layer,

11
00:00:34.746 --> 00:00:39.700
and the DNS identity and DNSSEC work
underneath certificates and tokens.

12
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.575
Part 2 covers building and running the
control plane: cloud private endpoints,

13
00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:49.225
the Azure DNS Private Resolver, IPAM
as the authority for address blocks,

14
00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:53.575
the reference architecture and its
five decisions, then the six-phase

15
00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:58.225
migration and the telemetry, drift
checks and evidence that keep it honest.

16
00:00:58.225 --> 00:01:03.525
Vendors and products appear only as the
book's examples, never as endorsements.

17
00:01:03.525 --> 00:01:07.330
The commands stay in the
chapters, marked illustrative.

18
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.268
Why this matters.

19
00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:13.267
Almost every network connection
begins with a DNS query,

20
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:18.968
and in a hybrid multi-cloud agency the
answer now decides far more than an address.

21
00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:24.472
NIST's 2026 DNS deployment guide,
SP 800-81 Revision 3, says DNS

22
00:01:24.472 --> 00:01:31.406
infrastructure has become more than a
service; it is also a security control.

23
00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:35.555
Chapter 01 names six jobs DNS now does.

24
00:01:35.555 --> 00:01:40.309
It publishes private addresses: an Azure
Storage account with a private endpoint

25
00:01:40.309 --> 00:01:45.342
resolves privately inside the hosting
virtual network and publicly everywhere else.

26
00:01:45.342 --> 00:01:51.209
It selects SaaS front doors: Microsoft
365 publishes a Worldwide list,

27
00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:57.379
which includes GCC, and separate lists
and namespaces for GCC High and DoD.

28
00:01:57.379 --> 00:02:02.867
It enforces security policy through
response policy zones and DNS firewalls.

29
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:08.654
It anchors certificate validation,
because OCSP and CRL endpoints are names.

30
00:02:08.654 --> 00:02:11.978
And the FQDN is the service
identity that gateways,

31
00:02:11.978 --> 00:02:15.855
certificates and Zero Trust
policies are written against.

32
00:02:15.855 --> 00:02:20.655
Yet most agencies run it on as many as
five platforms that share no state.

33
00:02:20.655 --> 00:02:24.505
AD DNS or a DDI grid on premises.

34
00:02:24.505 --> 00:02:29.730
Route 53's VPC Resolver,
formerly Route 53 Resolver.

35
00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:34.105
Azure, where private zones
resolve only for linked networks,

36
00:02:34.105 --> 00:02:38.305
and where DNS resolver policy
now gives an RPZ equivalent.

37
00:02:38.305 --> 00:02:40.380
Google Cloud's metadata server.

38
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.655
And the NSX forwarder, which
workloads reach only if configured to.

39
00:02:44.655 --> 00:02:47.180
The cost shows up on every change.

40
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:51.619
A new Azure private endpoint
can need four more DNS changes,

41
00:02:51.619 --> 00:02:54.155
on four consoles, in four syntaxes.

42
00:02:54.155 --> 00:02:57.868
Chapter 09 assigns ownership,
because the forwarding rules

43
00:02:57.868 --> 00:03:01.950
that connect the platforms
usually belong to no single team.

44
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.350
Chapter 03 quotes the mandates
rather than paraphrasing

45
00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.012
them into something
stronger, and so will I.

46
00:03:09.012 --> 00:03:15.417
OMB M-22-09 says agencies must resolve DNS
queries using encrypted DNS wherever it

47
00:03:15.417 --> 00:03:20.607
is technically supported, and should
explicitly configure endpoints to use

48
00:03:20.607 --> 00:03:23.931
agency-designated encrypted
DNS servers rather

49
00:03:23.931 --> 00:03:27.012
than relying on automatic
network discovery.

50
00:03:27.012 --> 00:03:31.939
CISA's Protective DNS is required
for federal civilian agencies,

51
00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:36.775
and CISA's guidance says it is used
for all egress DNS resolution.

52
00:03:36.775 --> 00:03:41.107
OMB M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31
and its event-logging tiers;

53
00:03:41.107 --> 00:03:43.033
it doesn't name DNS at all,

54
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:49.195
but its baseline needs source and
destination network address information,

55
00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:54.875
and logs actively searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year.

56
00:03:54.875 --> 00:03:59.477
M-08-23 required DNSSEC by December 2009,

57
00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:07.287
and SC-20 and SC-21 carry signing
and validation into every baseline.

58
00:04:07.287 --> 00:04:12.900
M-21-07's IPv6-only
targets have all passed.

59
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:19.863
And TIC 3.0, version 3.3, lists
five DNS enforcement capabilities.

60
00:04:19.863 --> 00:04:23.642
NIST SP 800-81 Revision
3, from March 2026,

61
00:04:23.642 --> 00:04:29.826
is the practical guide to meeting
them; it's guidance, not a mandate.

62
00:04:29.826 --> 00:04:34.916
Read together: agency-controlled
resolvers that log, encrypt, validate,

63
00:04:34.916 --> 00:04:39.575
forward egress through Protective
DNS, and can't be routed around.

64
00:04:39.575 --> 00:04:43.120
FedRAMP certifies a cloud
service, not an architecture,

65
00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.125
and the agency still
authorizes its own system.

66
00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:51.519
As an example, the Marketplace lists
Infoblox Government Cloud at Class C,

67
00:04:51.519 --> 00:04:55.675
its label for Moderate, delivered
as SaaS in AWS GovCloud.

68
00:04:55.675 --> 00:04:59.151
A Moderate certification
doesn't cover High or DoD,

69
00:04:59.151 --> 00:05:03.537
and on-premises appliances sit
inside the agency's own boundary.

70
00:05:03.537 --> 00:05:09.834
Every chapter follows one sequence:
inventory, coexist, migrate authority,

71
00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:15.825
federate clouds, decommission AD DNS
and DHCP, then harden and evidence.

72
00:05:15.825 --> 00:05:20.315
Five configuration decisions carry
most of the risk along the way;

73
00:05:20.315 --> 00:05:22.800
we'll meet each one in its module.

74
00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:29.050
Module 1: split-brain DNS and the
Active Directory DNS departure.

75
00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.332
Chapter 02 defines the structural problem:
the same name returning different answers

76
00:05:34.332 --> 00:05:39.100
depending on where the query starts,
silently, because the name still resolves.

77
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.550
Chapter 04 takes the surface
most agencies underestimate.

78
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:47.945
Active Directory never presented
itself as the agency's DNS service,

79
00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:49.872
but for twenty years it was one,

80
00:05:49.872 --> 00:05:54.575
and the records it kept for itself don't
follow the identities to the cloud.

81
00:05:54.575 --> 00:05:59.179
Leaving AD DNS is an inventory problem
before it is a migration problem,

82
00:05:59.179 --> 00:06:04.910
and the inventory has to be finished before
the first domain controller is retired.

83
00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.341
Split-brain is the same
name returning different

84
00:06:08.341 --> 00:06:11.022
answers depending on where
the query originates.

85
00:06:11.022 --> 00:06:15.384
Intentional split-horizon, designed
and governed, is legitimate.

86
00:06:15.384 --> 00:06:20.355
The unintentional kind is the problem,
and with several DNS systems holding

87
00:06:20.355 --> 00:06:24.772
copies of the same names and no shared
state, it's close to guaranteed.

88
00:06:24.772 --> 00:06:27.436
Chapter 02 shows three forms.

89
00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:29.085
Private-IP split-brain.

90
00:06:29.085 --> 00:06:34.773
An Azure Storage private endpoint
gives the public name a CNAME into the

91
00:06:34.773 --> 00:06:39.798
privatelink subdomain, and the private
zone maps it to, say, 10.1.0.4.

92
00:06:39.798 --> 00:06:44.679
An EC2 instance sees that only if
a Route 53 outbound rule forwards

93
00:06:44.679 --> 00:06:49.738
blob.core.windows.net to an Azure
Private Resolver inbound endpoint,

94
00:06:49.738 --> 00:06:52.223
or to an agency resolver that does.

95
00:06:52.223 --> 00:06:57.610
If the rule is missing or stale, the name
still resolves, to the public address.

96
00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.408
Microsoft's guide to 403 errors
through a private endpoint

97
00:07:01.408 --> 00:07:05.360
attributes roughly seventy
percent of cases to exactly that.

98
00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.085
Public SaaS split-brain.

99
00:07:07.085 --> 00:07:09.717
GCC uses the Worldwide endpoint list,

100
00:07:09.717 --> 00:07:14.047
so for a GCC-Moderate agency the
risk isn't a wrong-cloud answer;

101
00:07:14.047 --> 00:07:18.292
it's path consistency: every
reachable resolver must return

102
00:07:18.292 --> 00:07:22.198
what Microsoft publishes,
through the designed egress.

103
00:07:22.198 --> 00:07:25.673
GCC High and DoD have
their own namespaces.

104
00:07:25.673 --> 00:07:29.672
Drift looks like sign-in failures
or Conditional Access denials.

105
00:07:29.672 --> 00:07:31.610
Security policy split-brain.

106
00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.674
Block lists apply only at
the resolver that answers,

107
00:07:34.674 --> 00:07:37.881
and the cloud equivalents
are configured per network,

108
00:07:37.881 --> 00:07:41.872
so the effective posture is the
weakest resolver a client can reach.

109
00:07:41.872 --> 00:07:45.651
That matters beyond compliance:
NIST notes DNS servers can

110
00:07:45.651 --> 00:07:49.660
often prevent security incidents
earlier than other systems.

111
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.323
Runbooks fail because
the failure is silent,

112
00:07:52.323 --> 00:07:56.210
the change rate outruns them, and
the systems behave differently.

113
00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.360
The fix is structural: a control plane.

114
00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.423
When a domain is deployed,
its DNS name becomes a zone,

115
00:08:03.423 --> 00:08:08.970
the domain controllers running the DNS Server
role become its authoritative servers,

116
00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:13.823
and every domain-joined machine is
pointed at them for all of its resolution.

117
00:08:13.823 --> 00:08:17.872
Nobody chose that as a DNS
architecture, but it is one.

118
00:08:17.872 --> 00:08:23.028
A zone stored in Active Directory exists
only on domain controllers that run DNS,

119
00:08:23.028 --> 00:08:28.030
and storing it there brings two things
a file-backed zone lacks: secure dynamic

120
00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.338
updates, and multi-master operation,

121
00:08:30.338 --> 00:08:33.647
where every hosting domain
controller is a primary.

122
00:08:33.647 --> 00:08:39.570
Replication rides on AD replication, and
the replication scope, exposed as Domain,

123
00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:44.797
Forest, Legacy or Custom, decides
which domain controllers hold the zone.

124
00:08:44.797 --> 00:08:48.985
The rest decomposes into
components, each needing a decision.

125
00:08:48.985 --> 00:08:52.402
The AD-integrated zones,
including underscore msdcs,

126
00:08:52.402 --> 00:08:56.052
lose multi-master and secure
updates with the directory,

127
00:08:56.052 --> 00:09:01.022
so each is re-created on a platform that
gives its clients what they still need.

128
00:09:01.022 --> 00:09:06.122
File-backed zones are the simplest to
move; watch clients pointed at a secondary.

129
00:09:06.122 --> 00:09:08.959
Stub zones usually
become forwarding rules.

130
00:09:08.959 --> 00:09:13.548
Conditional forwarders carry trusts,
partners and cloud namespaces.

131
00:09:13.548 --> 00:09:16.747
GlobalNames then depends
on suffix search lists.

132
00:09:16.747 --> 00:09:20.886
Reverse zones must align with
IPAM or fill with orphans.

133
00:09:20.886 --> 00:09:23.497
And signed zones move
on their own sequence.

134
00:09:23.497 --> 00:09:26.211
One correction: the
source whitepaper called

135
00:09:26.211 --> 00:09:28.923
conditional forwarders
not easily exported.

136
00:09:28.923 --> 00:09:34.360
Windows stores each one as a zone,
so Get-DNS-Server-Zone lists it.

137
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.960
Forwarders accumulate for partners,

138
00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.735
trusts and retired systems,
often with no record of why.

139
00:09:40.735 --> 00:09:44.950
Finding them is easy; deciding
which still matter is the work.

140
00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:49.693
Domain controllers advertise
themselves through DNS.

141
00:09:49.693 --> 00:09:55.075
Netlogon on each domain controller
registers a family of SRV and A records.

142
00:09:55.075 --> 00:09:59.501
A client finds a domain controller
by calling D S Get D C Name,

143
00:09:59.501 --> 00:10:03.745
which queries DNS for SRV
records, sends an LDAP UDP ping

144
00:10:03.745 --> 00:10:07.900
to the candidates and caches
the first one that answers.

145
00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.446
These records are dynamic:
Netlogon re-registers them,

146
00:10:11.446 --> 00:10:15.937
and they change as domain controllers
come and go and as sites change.

147
00:10:15.937 --> 00:10:17.913
Here's what fails without each.

148
00:10:17.913 --> 00:10:22.551
The LDAP record: domain join,
authentication and Group Policy.

149
00:10:22.551 --> 00:10:25.463
The Kerberos records: ticket acquisition,

150
00:10:25.463 --> 00:10:29.875
falling back to NTLM where it's
still allowed, otherwise failing.

151
00:10:29.875 --> 00:10:34.650
The global catalog records: universal
groups and forest-wide searches.

152
00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.024
The site records: clients reach a distant

153
00:10:37.024 --> 00:10:39.887
domain controller rather
than failing outright.

154
00:10:39.887 --> 00:10:43.312
The PDC record, and kpasswd
for password changes.

155
00:10:43.312 --> 00:10:45.951
Two sources make the inventory complete:

156
00:10:45.951 --> 00:10:49.598
the Netlogon dot DNS file each
domain controller writes,

157
00:10:49.598 --> 00:10:51.926
listing what it intends to register,

158
00:10:51.926 --> 00:10:56.505
which Microsoft points to for agencies
running AD on non-Microsoft DNS,

159
00:10:56.505 --> 00:11:01.238
and the underscore msdcs and underscore
sites folders of the domain zone.

160
00:11:01.238 --> 00:11:06.497
The program's assessment resolves each
critical record from every DNS server and

161
00:11:06.497 --> 00:11:09.281
every site, and Chapter
10 makes thirty days

162
00:11:09.281 --> 00:11:12.375
of clean resolution a
gate for decommissioning.

163
00:11:12.375 --> 00:11:16.818
Because they're registered dynamically,
these records can only live on

164
00:11:16.818 --> 00:11:21.412
a DNS service that accepts secure
registration from domain controllers.

165
00:11:21.412 --> 00:11:26.948
That's why they stay on AD DNS, or a DDI
platform that accepts GSS-TSIG updates,

166
00:11:26.948 --> 00:11:33.110
until the last domain controller goes,
and why Azure Private DNS can't host them.

167
00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:39.998
Clients keep their own records current
through dynamic update, RFC 2136.

168
00:11:39.998 --> 00:11:43.369
Secure dynamic update
authenticates the client and

169
00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:46.586
records each name's owner
in an access control list.

170
00:11:46.586 --> 00:11:51.372
It exists only for AD-integrated
zones, which default to secure-only.

171
00:11:51.372 --> 00:11:55.473
Any authenticated user can
create a new A or PTR record;

172
00:11:55.473 --> 00:11:59.847
once a name exists, only principals
on its ACL can change it.

173
00:11:59.847 --> 00:12:03.323
Windows signs these updates with GSS-TSIG.

174
00:12:03.323 --> 00:12:05.217
The DHCP server is part of this,

175
00:12:05.217 --> 00:12:09.234
and five settings decide whether
the records stay trustworthy.

176
00:12:09.234 --> 00:12:14.385
Name ownership: a DHCP server that performs
a secure update becomes the name's

177
00:12:14.385 --> 00:12:19.053
owner, so if its failover partner takes
over, the partner can't update it;

178
00:12:19.053 --> 00:12:22.272
both partners must use
the same DNS credentials.

179
00:12:22.272 --> 00:12:27.403
DnsUpdateProxy solves ownership by
leaving its members' records unsecured;

180
00:12:27.403 --> 00:12:30.610
Microsoft warns against
it in secure-only zones.

181
00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:34.065
A dedicated credential is
the recommended pattern:

182
00:12:34.065 --> 00:12:37.360
one standard user account
every DHCP server uses.

183
00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:42.411
It matters most when DHCP runs on a domain
controller, which would otherwise lend

184
00:12:42.411 --> 00:12:47.160
its authority over every record, and it
isn't backed up with the DHCP database.

185
00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.716
Name protection creates DHCID records,

186
00:12:49.716 --> 00:12:54.673
so an update fails rather than overwrites
when another client holds the name.

187
00:12:54.673 --> 00:12:58.973
And scavenging: without it DNS becomes
a map of where devices used to be,

188
00:12:58.973 --> 00:13:03.942
and Microsoft documents that when scavenging
and lease durations disagree, you

189
00:13:03.942 --> 00:13:09.059
get duplicate records and Kerberos tickets
for one computer delivered to another.

190
00:13:09.059 --> 00:13:12.425
These settings are rarely
documented outside the servers,

191
00:13:12.425 --> 00:13:14.210
so they belong in the inventory.

192
00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:18.100
Chapter 04 gives read-only
cmdlets for each.

193
00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:24.675
Microsoft's guidance is a phased
source-of-authority conversion toward cloud-first

194
00:13:24.675 --> 00:13:28.503
and A D-minimized states, not
an all-at-once decommission,

195
00:13:28.503 --> 00:13:34.162
and AD DS itself isn't deprecated: Windows
Server 2025 ships new functional levels.

196
00:13:34.162 --> 00:13:37.413
What changes is where the
DNS records come from.

197
00:13:37.413 --> 00:13:41.626
Entra ID publishes no zones
and no locator records.

198
00:13:41.626 --> 00:13:46.088
Entra-joined devices use tokens and
don't look for a domain controller;

199
00:13:46.088 --> 00:13:48.088
hybrid-joined devices still do.

200
00:13:48.088 --> 00:13:53.304
Kerberos survives in pockets, such as
Application Proxy with constrained

201
00:13:53.304 --> 00:13:57.763
delegation and cloud Kerberos
trust, each with a DNS dependency.

202
00:13:57.763 --> 00:14:01.299
And Entra Domain Services
brings its own DNS,

203
00:14:01.299 --> 00:14:05.506
needs conditional forwarders
for on-premises zones,

204
00:14:05.506 --> 00:14:11.243
and Microsoft says any server-level
forwarder other than 168.63.129.16 is

205
00:14:11.243 --> 00:14:16.023
unsupported: a DNS authority
whose upstream you can't change,

206
00:14:16.023 --> 00:14:18.700
which matters for Protective DNS.

207
00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:22.625
Kerberos needs more from
DNS than tickets reveal.

208
00:14:22.625 --> 00:14:27.482
The source whitepaper described the
service resolving the SPN back to an IP;

209
00:14:27.482 --> 00:14:30.875
Kerberos does no such check,
and the book corrected it.

210
00:14:30.875 --> 00:14:35.748
The real failure is simpler and worse:
resolve the name to the wrong host and

211
00:14:35.748 --> 00:14:40.463
a valid ticket for computer A is
presented to computer B, which rejects it.

212
00:14:40.463 --> 00:14:44.225
A missing SPN silently drops to NTLM.

213
00:14:44.225 --> 00:14:48.144
Either way it looks like
identity, and the cause is DNS.

214
00:14:48.144 --> 00:14:52.598
Decoupling DNS from identity
also removes a shared failure:

215
00:14:52.598 --> 00:14:56.963
an identity outage no longer
takes name resolution with it.

216
00:14:56.963 --> 00:14:59.318
Before any domain controller goes:

217
00:14:59.318 --> 00:15:04.029
keep locator records resolvable
until every consumer is token-based;

218
00:15:04.029 --> 00:15:08.821
test conditional forwarders over DNS,
not ICMP, and retire the dead ones;

219
00:15:08.821 --> 00:15:14.426
create the internal namespace explicitly;
re-establish dynamic registration per

220
00:15:14.426 --> 00:15:17.187
client population; and move GlobalNames,

221
00:15:17.187 --> 00:15:20.274
reverse and signed zones
by their own methods.

222
00:15:20.274 --> 00:15:24.060
DDI platforms commonly
offer a coexistence mode;

223
00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.738
Infoblox NIOS is the book's example.

224
00:15:26.738 --> 00:15:29.850
Confirm the capability
for whatever you deploy.

225
00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:35.738
Module 2: edge interception,
DNS identity and DNSSEC.

226
00:15:35.738 --> 00:15:40.797
Every control in the policy plane,
response policy zones, DNSSEC validation,

227
00:15:40.797 --> 00:15:45.375
logging and Protective DNS forwarding,
assumes the query reaches it.

228
00:15:45.375 --> 00:15:50.049
Chapter 05 takes the three ordinary
mechanisms that decide whether it does,

229
00:15:50.049 --> 00:15:53.313
at the network edge, the
endpoint and the access layer,

230
00:15:53.313 --> 00:15:55.687
and none of them reports when it doesn't.

231
00:15:55.687 --> 00:15:59.200
Chapter 06 then shows why
a certificate can be valid,

232
00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.877
unrevoked and issued for the
right name and still fail:

233
00:16:02.877 --> 00:16:07.125
every check that makes it
trustworthy starts with a DNS lookup.

234
00:16:07.125 --> 00:16:09.150
It closes with DNSSEC.

235
00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.841
SD-WAN edges can intercept DNS regardless

236
00:16:12.841 --> 00:16:15.950
of the resolver a client
was configured with.

237
00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:19.373
In Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN,
the book's worked example,

238
00:16:19.373 --> 00:16:23.547
this is the redirect-dns action
of a centralized data policy:

239
00:16:23.547 --> 00:16:28.639
the edge matches DNS traffic and sends
it to a policy-designated resolver,

240
00:16:28.639 --> 00:16:32.563
either unconditionally, or
only for named applications.

241
00:16:32.563 --> 00:16:36.325
The destination decides whether
agency policy applies at all.

242
00:16:36.325 --> 00:16:41.302
On the correct path the query goes through
the edge to a network-addressable agency

243
00:16:41.302 --> 00:16:44.764
resolver, into the policy plane
for response policy zones,

244
00:16:44.764 --> 00:16:49.525
validation and logging, and internet
recursion leaves through Protective DNS.

245
00:16:49.525 --> 00:16:55.078
On the bypassed path the edge hands it
to an external resolver: no log entry,

246
00:16:55.078 --> 00:17:00.175
no RPZ, no validation under agency
trust anchors, and nothing alerts.

247
00:17:00.175 --> 00:17:02.287
The feature has documented limits.

248
00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:07.813
Service-side redirect
arrived in IOS XE 17.8.1a.

249
00:17:07.813 --> 00:17:12.054
It's IPv4 and UDP only, so
TCP DNS isn't redirected,

250
00:17:12.054 --> 00:17:18.362
and it skips overlay-tunnel DNS,
fragments and the router's own queries.

251
00:17:18.362 --> 00:17:23.175
Cisco's Umbrella integration is a
separate mechanism whose local bypass

252
00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:27.988
list is effectively a second copy of
the internal namespace on every edge.

253
00:17:27.988 --> 00:17:29.300
Two refinements.

254
00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.670
The target must be a server the edge
can address; a loopback agent such as

255
00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:38.263
Infoblox Endpoint protects the roaming
device but isn't a redirect target.

256
00:17:38.263 --> 00:17:43.601
And a SASE resolver, even Cisco's Umbrella
for Government in an offering listed

257
00:17:43.601 --> 00:17:48.454
FedRAMP Certified at High, which
describes Protective DNS integration,

258
00:17:48.454 --> 00:17:50.962
still bypasses the agency's own plane.

259
00:17:50.962 --> 00:17:54.629
So the redirect target is
always the agency resolver,

260
00:17:54.629 --> 00:17:56.962
with any SASE resolver behind it.

261
00:17:56.962 --> 00:18:02.166
Microsoft 365 informed network
routing supports Worldwide

262
00:18:02.166 --> 00:18:06.962
commercial tenants only, not
GCC Moderate, GCC High or DoD.

263
00:18:06.962 --> 00:18:11.160
It's a traffic optimization,
not a DNS dependency.

264
00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:17.385
Client-side encrypted DNS to a public
resolver is the most common bypass.

265
00:18:17.385 --> 00:18:24.040
M-22-09 wants encrypted DNS wherever
supported and endpoints set explicitly to

266
00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:28.879
agency resolvers; CISA's target
state has Protective DNS upstream

267
00:18:28.879 --> 00:18:33.460
and endpoints unable to reach
third-party DNS over any protocol.

268
00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:39.572
So encrypted DNS must terminate at an
agency resolver or Protective DNS.

269
00:18:39.572 --> 00:18:41.922
Each transport leaves a different handle.

270
00:18:41.922 --> 00:18:47.522
Plain DNS on 53 can be intercepted,
redirected and blocked by port.

271
00:18:47.522 --> 00:18:53.930
DNS over TLS and DNS over QUIC, on port
853, can't be redirected without breaking

272
00:18:53.930 --> 00:19:00.236
the session, but are straightforward
to block except to approved resolvers.

273
00:19:00.236 --> 00:19:06.439
DNS over HTTPS shares 443 with all web
traffic, so redirect-dns can't see it;

274
00:19:06.439 --> 00:19:12.125
NIST suggests RPZ entries for known
DoH names plus firewall rules,

275
00:19:12.125 --> 00:19:14.710
and CISA adds TLS inspection.

276
00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.410
An edge-only design can't
govern encrypted DNS.

277
00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:23.141
On Windows, the client uses DoH only
with servers on its known list,

278
00:19:23.141 --> 00:19:27.873
which ships with Cloudflare, Google
and Quad9; add the agency's own.

279
00:19:27.873 --> 00:19:33.444
Since June 2026 the Windows Server
2025 DNS Server accepts client DoH,

280
00:19:33.444 --> 00:19:36.998
but its upstream traffic
stays unencrypted.

281
00:19:36.998 --> 00:19:42.245
CISA says IPsec isn't an acceptable
substitute, suggests an encrypting proxy,

282
00:19:42.245 --> 00:19:46.120
requires root hints disabled,
and allows public fallback

283
00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:50.560
resolvers only when Protective
DNS is unavailable, with alerting.

284
00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:54.538
Zero Trust DNS, on Windows
11 Enterprise and Education,

285
00:19:54.538 --> 00:20:00.548
permits outbound traffic only to addresses
its Protective DNS servers resolved.

286
00:20:00.548 --> 00:20:02.973
Browsers resolve names themselves.

287
00:20:02.973 --> 00:20:08.739
The program adopts CISA's configuration:
the OS resolver, or DoH only to agency

288
00:20:08.739 --> 00:20:14.772
resolvers, as mandatory policy, and an
NXDOMAIN answer for Firefox's canary domain.

289
00:20:14.772 --> 00:20:19.217
Microsoft documents that on a
managed device with the policy unset,

290
00:20:19.217 --> 00:20:21.260
Edge sends no DoH queries.

291
00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:24.698
The third bypass sits on the access layer.

292
00:20:24.698 --> 00:20:30.161
A rogue DHCP server that hands out a malicious
resolver in option 6 can move a whole

293
00:20:30.161 --> 00:20:33.215
subnet onto an attacker's
resolver in one step,

294
00:20:33.215 --> 00:20:37.635
past every response policy zone
and every Protective DNS control.

295
00:20:37.635 --> 00:20:42.254
MITRE ATT&CK catalogs it as
DHCP Spoofing, T1557.003;

296
00:20:42.254 --> 00:20:49.623
the whitepaper's claim of federal incidents
couldn't be verified and was removed.

297
00:20:49.623 --> 00:20:54.663
In IPv6, rogue router advertisements
carrying the RDNSS option do the same,

298
00:20:54.663 --> 00:20:57.102
and they're often the larger surface,

299
00:20:57.102 --> 00:21:01.410
because many networks govern
router advertisements not at all.

300
00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:06.839
M-22-09's instruction to configure
agency resolvers explicitly

301
00:21:06.839 --> 00:21:10.910
is the federal answer to
discovery channels like RFC 9463.

302
00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:14.335
Option 6 isn't the only
option that moves traffic.

303
00:21:14.335 --> 00:21:19.072
Option 15, the default domain,
must match the authoritative zone.

304
00:21:19.072 --> 00:21:23.772
Option 119, the search list,
misdirects queries when stale.

305
00:21:23.772 --> 00:21:27.509
Option 81 decides who
registers the client's records.

306
00:21:27.509 --> 00:21:32.098
Option 82 identifies the switch
port for IPAM attribution.

307
00:21:32.098 --> 00:21:36.436
And 252 for WPAD is a
convention, not an assignment;

308
00:21:36.436 --> 00:21:39.611
a wrong value bypasses the web proxy.

309
00:21:39.611 --> 00:21:42.360
The Windows control is
narrower than it looks.

310
00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:45.718
An unauthorized Windows
DHCP server checks its

311
00:21:45.718 --> 00:21:49.160
own status in Active
Directory and stops leasing.

312
00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:52.204
That stops a misconfigured Windows server,

313
00:21:52.204 --> 00:21:56.348
not a rogue appliance, a home
router in a wall port, or malware.

314
00:21:56.348 --> 00:22:00.335
Those need DHCP snooping and RA
Guard on the access switches,

315
00:22:00.335 --> 00:22:04.242
centralized scope and option
management, rogue detection,

316
00:22:04.242 --> 00:22:08.548
and IPAM integration so an
unexpected lease is visible at once.

317
00:22:08.548 --> 00:22:12.132
Prevention is never complete,
so watch for failure:

318
00:22:12.132 --> 00:22:17.338
hosts whose queries never reach the
governed logs, fallback resolver use,

319
00:22:17.338 --> 00:22:22.740
port 853 to unapproved destinations,
and DoH to known public endpoints.

320
00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:28.198
DNS identity, the authoritative
mapping of names to addresses,

321
00:22:28.198 --> 00:22:32.453
owners and lifecycle, is the
first link in the trust chain.

322
00:22:32.453 --> 00:22:36.919
Certificates, FQDN-based policy
and tokens all name their

323
00:22:36.919 --> 00:22:41.014
subject by FQDN and trust
DNS to take the caller there.

324
00:22:41.014 --> 00:22:44.601
Moving from Kerberos to
Entra ID tokens removes the

325
00:22:44.601 --> 00:22:48.103
KDC but adds names that
must resolve consistently.

326
00:22:48.103 --> 00:22:54.111
The token endpoint: GCC uses the global
login dot microsoftonline dot com;

327
00:22:54.111 --> 00:22:59.528
Azure Government, serving GCC High
and DoD, uses the dot us endpoint.

328
00:22:59.528 --> 00:23:03.752
The whitepaper grouped GCC with
dot us; the book corrected it.

329
00:23:03.752 --> 00:23:08.700
The token audience in v2.0 tokens
is the API's client ID, a GUID,

330
00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:15.264
so a host rename clients don't pick up
fails as an audience or redirect-URI error.

331
00:23:15.264 --> 00:23:18.403
SANs must contain the name
the client asked for.

332
00:23:18.403 --> 00:23:23.685
Entra certificate-based
authentication checks one HTTP

333
00:23:23.685 --> 00:23:27.977
CRL per CA and no OCSP, so
the CRL URL must resolve.

334
00:23:27.977 --> 00:23:32.887
And ACME dns-01 makes the zone
the gate for automated issuance,

335
00:23:32.887 --> 00:23:38.952
exercised constantly as public TLS
lifetimes fall toward forty-seven days.

336
00:23:38.952 --> 00:23:45.753
Certificate split-brain: a service's
OCSP and CRL names exist only in AD DNS,

337
00:23:45.753 --> 00:23:49.878
and an EC2 caller has no
Route 53 rule for them.

338
00:23:49.878 --> 00:23:54.353
A hard-fail client breaks the handshake;
a soft-fail client skips the check.

339
00:23:54.353 --> 00:23:58.815
The agency resolver logs nothing,
and the ticket goes to PKI.

340
00:23:58.815 --> 00:24:00.228
That's Decision 5.

341
00:24:00.228 --> 00:24:06.163
Geographic scoping: RFC 9525 allows a
certificate wildcard only as the whole

342
00:24:06.163 --> 00:24:08.853
leftmost label, matching one label,

343
00:24:08.853 --> 00:24:13.027
so application dot star dot
agency dot gov doesn't work.

344
00:24:13.027 --> 00:24:17.681
Use per-region names, per-region
SANs, and name constraints,

345
00:24:17.681 --> 00:24:22.431
the only cryptographic layer;
RFC 5280 says clients should,

346
00:24:22.431 --> 00:24:26.860
not must, process them, so
test your relying parties.

347
00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:30.279
DNSSEC adds data-origin authentication,

348
00:24:30.279 --> 00:24:34.360
integrity protection and
authenticated denial of existence.

349
00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:39.082
It doesn't encrypt anything; it lets a
resolver verify the answer is authentic,

350
00:24:39.082 --> 00:24:43.435
and a validating resolver that finds
a bad signature answers SERVFAIL.

351
00:24:43.435 --> 00:24:49.068
NIST is explicit that DNSSEC and encrypted
DNS are complements: DNSSEC doesn't

352
00:24:49.068 --> 00:24:51.495
protect confidentiality, and DoH,

353
00:24:51.495 --> 00:24:55.222
DoT and DoQ protect the
transaction but not the data.

354
00:24:55.222 --> 00:24:56.586
The federal position.

355
00:24:56.586 --> 00:25:04.635
M-08-23 required DNSSEC by December
2009, with agency dot gov domains signed.

356
00:25:04.635 --> 00:25:10.084
SC-20 asks authoritative services
for origin and integrity artifacts,

357
00:25:10.084 --> 00:25:15.810
and SC-21 asks the recursive resolver
to request and perform verification.

358
00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:18.998
SP 800-81 Revision 3 recommends the root

359
00:25:18.998 --> 00:25:22.573
key as trust anchor with
automated rollover.

360
00:25:22.573 --> 00:25:25.949
On internal zones it's more
cautious than the whitepaper:

361
00:25:25.949 --> 00:25:30.833
signing them is likely of limited value
and recommended against unless required,

362
00:25:30.833 --> 00:25:35.287
because they may not chain to the root,
dynamic zones re-sign on every update,

363
00:25:35.287 --> 00:25:38.303
and signing is pointless
where authoritative and

364
00:25:38.303 --> 00:25:41.823
recursive service share servers,
as on domain controllers.

365
00:25:41.823 --> 00:25:45.385
Where required, use a delegation
that chains to the root.

366
00:25:45.385 --> 00:25:50.488
So the program signs external zones
always, validates on every agency resolver,

367
00:25:50.488 --> 00:25:55.360
and signs internal zones only where
the system's authorization requires it.

368
00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:56.985
The platforms aren't equal.

369
00:25:56.985 --> 00:26:01.985
Windows Server uses a Key
Master, can replicate keys in AD,

370
00:26:01.985 --> 00:26:08.794
and supports RFC 5011 rollover; its
wizard's default 1,024-bit RSA zone-signing

371
00:26:08.794 --> 00:26:15.497
key is below NIST's floor, so the program
uses ECDSA or RSA of at least 2,048 bits.

372
00:26:15.497 --> 00:26:20.612
Azure DNS and Route 53 sign public
zones only; Azure's default

373
00:26:20.612 --> 00:26:26.135
resolver doesn't validate, and Route
53's ignores the DO and CD bits.

374
00:26:26.135 --> 00:26:29.540
Neither gives the agency
validation it controls.

375
00:26:30.290 --> 00:26:34.702
Operating DNSSEC is mostly key
management, automated and audited.

376
00:26:34.702 --> 00:26:40.050
NIST recommends a hardware security module
for important keys, usually the KSK,

377
00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:43.210
signature validity of
about five to seven days,

378
00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:45.803
and key lifetimes of one to three years.

379
00:26:45.803 --> 00:26:54.107
Its acceptable algorithms include RSA with
SHA-256 at 2,048 bits or more and ECDSA

380
00:26:54.107 --> 00:27:02.040
P-256 and P-384, and it prefers ECDSA,
because smaller keys keep responses small.

381
00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:07.444
On denial of existence, Windows
labels NSEC3 recommended,

382
00:27:07.444 --> 00:27:14.762
while NIST notes NSEC3 relies on SHA-1
and calls NSEC the more prudent approach;

383
00:27:14.762 --> 00:27:20.278
compact denial of existence, RFC
9824, is what Azure DNS uses.

384
00:27:20.278 --> 00:27:25.603
Signing grows responses, so EDNS zero
and fragmentation handling matter.

385
00:27:25.603 --> 00:27:28.199
And a zone signed in
one view but not another

386
00:27:28.199 --> 00:27:30.940
fails as an application
or certificate error.

387
00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:34.958
Moving a signed zone has its own
sequence, run during Chapter 10's

388
00:27:34.958 --> 00:27:38.977
Phase 3 so the new chain validates
before the decommission gate.

389
00:27:38.977 --> 00:27:43.477
First, finish any rollover in progress
and start none during the move.

390
00:27:43.477 --> 00:27:47.111
Second, keep the old signing
until the new chain validates:

391
00:27:47.111 --> 00:27:50.593
sign on the new platform,
publish the new DS at the parent,

392
00:27:50.593 --> 00:27:55.590
confirm from an independent validator,
and only then stop signing at the source.

393
00:27:55.590 --> 00:28:00.577
Third, watch the validators, because
clients see only generic failures.

394
00:28:00.577 --> 00:28:05.149
Fourth, an AD-signed internal zone
landing on Azure Private DNS,

395
00:28:05.149 --> 00:28:08.490
which can't sign, is a
documented risk decision.

396
00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:12.209
The book corrects two compensations
the source guide listed:

397
00:28:12.209 --> 00:28:15.709
Azure virtual network traffic
isn't encrypted by default,

398
00:28:15.709 --> 00:28:19.428
and Private Link doesn't make
in-network answers tamper-proof.

399
00:28:19.428 --> 00:28:24.276
Removing DNSSEC is the reverse:
Microsoft documents that the DS record

400
00:28:24.276 --> 00:28:28.878
comes off the parent and its TTL
expires before the zone is unsigned.

401
00:28:28.878 --> 00:28:34.090
Fifth, remove retired trust anchors
after the ninety-day validation period.

402
00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:38.003
Part 2: building and
running the control plane.

403
00:28:38.003 --> 00:28:40.214
Module 3 covers the design.

404
00:28:40.214 --> 00:28:45.675
Chapter 07's premise: every cloud ships
its own private DNS and its own address

405
00:28:45.675 --> 00:28:48.661
manager, and unless
one plane governs both,

406
00:28:48.661 --> 00:28:52.415
names and addresses drift
apart one console at a time.

407
00:28:52.415 --> 00:28:57.672
We'll take the four private DNS models,
Azure private endpoints and the Private

408
00:28:57.672 --> 00:29:03.415
Resolver in detail, then IPAM as the authority
for address blocks across the clouds.

409
00:29:03.415 --> 00:29:08.595
Chapter 08 then draws the reference
architecture and the five decisions that carry

410
00:29:08.595 --> 00:29:12.813
almost all of its operational risk,
each with a check to run in the live

411
00:29:12.813 --> 00:29:17.690
configuration, and Appendix C sets
out what changes in government clouds.

412
00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:23.475
Each cloud ships its own private DNS,
with its own console, API, zone model

413
00:29:23.475 --> 00:29:28.765
and failure behavior, and none was
designed to share policy with the others.

414
00:29:28.765 --> 00:29:34.341
AWS has Route 53 private hosted zones
answered by the VPC Resolver, inbound

415
00:29:34.341 --> 00:29:37.038
endpoints for queries from outside,

416
00:29:37.038 --> 00:29:41.265
outbound endpoints and Resolver
rules, and DNS Firewall.

417
00:29:41.265 --> 00:29:45.674
Azure has Private DNS zones
resolvable only from linked networks,

418
00:29:45.674 --> 00:29:50.965
the Private Resolver's endpoints and
forwarding rulesets, and resolver policy.

419
00:29:50.965 --> 00:29:55.826
Google Cloud has private zones per
VPC network, inbound server policies,

420
00:29:55.826 --> 00:29:59.390
forwarding and peering zones,
and response policies.

421
00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:02.015
VMware NSX has no private zone at all:

422
00:30:02.015 --> 00:30:06.740
a gateway forwarder holds a default
zone and up to five FQDN zones

423
00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:11.290
of three servers each, used only
by workloads configured for it.

424
00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:15.840
Azure private endpoints are the most
common source of private-IP split-brain.

425
00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:20.284
With private DNS integration, a zone
group writes the endpoint's A record

426
00:30:20.284 --> 00:30:24.803
into the matching privatelink zone
and removes it when the endpoint goes.

427
00:30:24.803 --> 00:30:28.381
Public DNS carries a CNAME
into the privatelink name,

428
00:30:28.381 --> 00:30:30.740
so connection strings don't change.

429
00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:34.915
The private answer comes back only from
a resolver that can see a linked zone.

430
00:30:34.915 --> 00:30:36.865
Three behaviors catch teams out.

431
00:30:36.865 --> 00:30:42.209
DNS answers don't grant access: the public
chain resolves from anywhere by design,

432
00:30:42.209 --> 00:30:45.080
and access is the
service's network setting,

433
00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:49.865
so a wrong answer shows up as a
public-path connection or an access denial.

434
00:30:49.865 --> 00:30:54.649
Use one zone per service type everywhere,
with Microsoft's recommended names,

435
00:30:54.649 --> 00:30:57.340
since records are
generated only for those.

436
00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:02.169
And the NXDOMAIN trap: a partner's storage
account with its own private endpoint

437
00:31:02.169 --> 00:31:04.364
leads into your empty zone and fails;

438
00:31:04.364 --> 00:31:07.650
the fix is fallback to
internet on the zone's link.

439
00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:11.127
Two rules turn Azure
private endpoints from a

440
00:31:11.127 --> 00:31:13.925
source of split-brain
into something governed.

441
00:31:13.925 --> 00:31:16.012
First, forward the public zone.

442
00:31:16.012 --> 00:31:20.573
Forwarders on non-Azure resolvers
point at each service's public zone,

443
00:31:20.573 --> 00:31:24.899
for example database dot windows
dot net, not the privatelink zone.

444
00:31:24.899 --> 00:31:28.476
Every origin, on-premises
resolvers, Route 53 rules,

445
00:31:28.476 --> 00:31:33.244
Cloud DNS forwarding zones and branch
paths, sends it to the Private

446
00:31:33.244 --> 00:31:38.437
Resolver's inbound endpoint, or to an
agency resolver that forwards there.

447
00:31:38.437 --> 00:31:40.878
Azure Government uses different names,

448
00:31:40.878 --> 00:31:43.837
so a design spanning both
clouds needs both sets.

449
00:31:43.837 --> 00:31:48.427
Forwarding a whole public zone also
carries other organizations' lookups,

450
00:31:48.427 --> 00:31:51.537
which is why fallback to
internet goes on the links.

451
00:31:51.537 --> 00:31:56.286
Autoregistration is separate:
VMs only, primary NIC only,

452
00:31:56.286 --> 00:32:00.226
no PTR records, and no
role in privatelink zones.

453
00:32:00.226 --> 00:32:05.163
Second, build the resolver in the hub as
a forwarding bridge, not an authority.

454
00:32:05.163 --> 00:32:09.174
Because it isn't authoritative,
on-premises DNS reaches

455
00:32:09.174 --> 00:32:13.012
it by conditional forwarding,
never by NS delegation.

456
00:32:13.012 --> 00:32:14.362
The rules that matter.

457
00:32:14.362 --> 00:32:17.283
Each endpoint gets its
own delegated subnet,

458
00:32:17.283 --> 00:32:19.888
slash twenty-eight to slash twenty-four.

459
00:32:19.888 --> 00:32:23.809
A resolver supports five inbound
and five outbound endpoints;

460
00:32:23.809 --> 00:32:27.731
a ruleset holds a thousand rules
across five hundred networks;

461
00:32:27.731 --> 00:32:31.049
each endpoint handles ten
thousand queries a second.

462
00:32:31.049 --> 00:32:36.112
No encrypted networks, IPv6
subnets, FastPath or Lighthouse.

463
00:32:36.112 --> 00:32:39.975
Static inbound addresses, so
forwarders are written once.

464
00:32:39.975 --> 00:32:43.122
Never link a ruleset to
the network whose inbound

465
00:32:43.122 --> 00:32:45.962
endpoint one of its rules
targets; that loops.

466
00:32:45.962 --> 00:32:50.523
Don't list a custom resolver
beside 168.63.129.16,

467
00:32:50.523 --> 00:32:55.200
because some clients pick
among listed servers.

468
00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:57.490
And a resolver in each region.

469
00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:01.528
The address side has the same
structure as the name side.

470
00:33:01.528 --> 00:33:03.950
AWS VPCs, Azure virtual networks,

471
00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:09.155
Google Cloud VPC networks and NSX
segments each allocate on their own,

472
00:33:09.155 --> 00:33:13.553
so a CIDR created in a console
can overlap on-premises space,

473
00:33:13.553 --> 00:33:17.860
another cloud or another
division, and the enterprise DDI

474
00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:22.527
system doesn't know until discovery
runs or a conflict appears.

475
00:33:22.527 --> 00:33:24.452
Integration depth differs.

476
00:33:24.452 --> 00:33:29.658
Amazon VPC IPAM can hand a private
scope's authority to an external IPAM;

477
00:33:29.658 --> 00:33:33.128
since November 2025 the
book's example is Infoblox,

478
00:33:33.128 --> 00:33:37.177
which chooses a block or accepts
or rejects a requested one.

479
00:33:37.177 --> 00:33:41.199
It needs the Advanced Tier, covers
private scopes only, and isn't

480
00:33:41.199 --> 00:33:45.839
available in GovCloud or China, so it
doesn't help a GovCloud estate today.

481
00:33:45.839 --> 00:33:51.524
Azure Virtual Network Manager offers
hierarchical pools, a root and up to

482
00:33:51.524 --> 00:33:56.928
seven layers, IPv4 and IPv6, with
static CIDRs for ranges outside Azure.

483
00:33:56.928 --> 00:34:00.152
Google Cloud's internal
ranges can reserve blocks.

484
00:34:00.152 --> 00:34:03.188
Neither hands authority
to an external IPAM,

485
00:34:03.188 --> 00:34:07.314
so mirror the enterprise reservations
into them and reconcile.

486
00:34:07.314 --> 00:34:13.766
VMware Cloud Foundation 9.1 added native
Infoblox allocation and FQDN registration;

487
00:34:13.766 --> 00:34:19.490
the source is a vendor blog without IPv6
or limits, so confirm the scope first.

488
00:34:19.490 --> 00:34:24.942
The rule: reserve blocks in the enterprise
IPAM before any cloud or NSX network is

489
00:34:24.942 --> 00:34:27.076
created, integrate where you can,

490
00:34:27.076 --> 00:34:31.027
mirror and reconcile where you
can't, and monitor sync health.

491
00:34:31.027 --> 00:34:35.780
Federation plus active and passive
discovery produce one hierarchy,

492
00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:41.516
which catches overlap before two networks
are peered and reports the CM-8 inventory.

493
00:34:41.516 --> 00:34:47.403
The program also wants IPAM to track
FQDNs, wildcards and bound certificates.

494
00:34:47.403 --> 00:34:52.866
IPv6: M-21-07's milestones have passed.

495
00:34:52.866 --> 00:34:59.620
Plan both protocols together; the Private
Resolver can't use IPv6-enabled subnets.

496
00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:03.295
Chapter 08's architecture
is simple to draw.

497
00:35:03.295 --> 00:35:08.253
A branch query goes through the SD-WAN
edge to a network-addressable agency

498
00:35:08.253 --> 00:35:12.571
resolver, into the policy plane
for policy, response policy zones,

499
00:35:12.571 --> 00:35:16.170
DNSSEC and IPAM, and on to
the per-cloud private zones.

500
00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:21.139
Internet-bound recursion leaves
through CISA's Protective DNS Resolver,

501
00:35:21.139 --> 00:35:26.108
which blocks, redirects or sinkholes
queries matching threat indicators.

502
00:35:26.108 --> 00:35:29.854
On-premises clients reach
the agency resolver directly;

503
00:35:29.854 --> 00:35:34.796
cloud workloads reach the plane through
Chapter 07's per-cloud forwarding.

504
00:35:34.796 --> 00:35:40.320
Five decisions carry almost all of the
operational risk, and each fails silently:

505
00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:45.020
the query still resolves, just outside
policy or to the wrong address.

506
00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:48.591
So each has a check to run
in the live configuration,

507
00:35:48.591 --> 00:35:52.757
not the design document, before
the next SD-WAN or DDI change.

508
00:35:52.757 --> 00:35:58.018
Decision 1, the redirect-dns destination:
a DDI resolver or forwarding

509
00:35:58.018 --> 00:36:02.371
proxy at the branch or a regional
hub, never a loopback stub.

510
00:36:02.371 --> 00:36:04.982
Check every branch's running data policy.

511
00:36:04.982 --> 00:36:08.745
Decision 2, forwarding chain
order: a SASE resolver is a

512
00:36:08.745 --> 00:36:13.407
forwarding target of the agency
resolver, never the redirect target.

513
00:36:13.407 --> 00:36:16.339
The common failure is
every component present

514
00:36:16.339 --> 00:36:19.270
with the SASE service
first, so trace the order.

515
00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:23.846
Decision 3, per-cloud conditional
forwarding: the plane holds every

516
00:36:23.846 --> 00:36:28.995
private namespace's rule and pushes it
to every resolver, branches included.

517
00:36:28.995 --> 00:36:32.831
Check: resolve one name per
namespace from on-premises,

518
00:36:32.831 --> 00:36:37.945
each cloud and a branch; a public
address or NXDOMAIN means a missing rule.

519
00:36:37.945 --> 00:36:44.004
Decision 4, the AD DNS inventory, reprovisioned
before any domain controller goes,

520
00:36:44.004 --> 00:36:49.707
because once the last one holding a zone
is gone, its records can't be recovered.

521
00:36:49.707 --> 00:36:53.270
Check for a dated, reconciled
export before approval.

522
00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:58.470
Decision 5, OCSP and CRL
reachability from every origin.

523
00:36:58.470 --> 00:37:04.700
Check: resolve each endpoint in the issuing
CA certificates, and fetch a response.

524
00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:07.750
The policy plane isn't one box.

525
00:37:07.750 --> 00:37:11.150
It has three parts, which
products package differently.

526
00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:17.147
An on-premises DNS and DHCP layer hosts
the internal zones that replace AD DNS,

527
00:37:17.147 --> 00:37:22.113
serves recursion, and applies
response policy zones and DNSSEC.

528
00:37:22.113 --> 00:37:24.483
Grid members answer from local copies,

529
00:37:24.483 --> 00:37:29.374
so the program's position is that changes
converge in seconds, not milliseconds,

530
00:37:29.374 --> 00:37:34.562
and an emergency block must be verified on
the members serving the affected clients.

531
00:37:34.562 --> 00:37:37.736
A management plane,
increasingly a cloud service,

532
00:37:37.736 --> 00:37:41.063
holds the system of record
and pushes it everywhere.

533
00:37:41.063 --> 00:37:44.019
And local resolvers and
agents keep resolving

534
00:37:44.019 --> 00:37:46.975
from local state if
management is unreachable.

535
00:37:46.975 --> 00:37:49.236
It's overlay, not replacement:

536
00:37:49.236 --> 00:37:53.475
Route 53 and Azure Private
DNS still serve their zones.

537
00:37:53.475 --> 00:37:59.913
Infoblox is the book's example: the NIOS
grid, and Universal DDI as the cloud plane.

538
00:37:59.913 --> 00:38:05.261
Its FedRAMP-listed offering is Infoblox
Government Cloud, certified at Moderate;

539
00:38:05.261 --> 00:38:08.172
on-premises NIOS appliances
aren't a FedRAMP

540
00:38:08.172 --> 00:38:11.475
cloud service and sit inside
the agency's boundary.

541
00:38:11.475 --> 00:38:15.863
Chapter 12 evaluates other platforms
against the same requirements.

542
00:38:15.863 --> 00:38:21.100
The architecture is the same everywhere;
authorization and integrations change.

543
00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:25.127
A plane certified at Moderate, plus
the agency's own authorization,

544
00:38:25.127 --> 00:38:29.887
suffices for Moderate systems but doesn't
cover High: use a service authorized

545
00:38:29.887 --> 00:38:33.987
at the required level or keep the
plane inside the agency's boundary.

546
00:38:33.987 --> 00:38:36.176
Azure Government's zone names differ.

547
00:38:36.176 --> 00:38:40.000
VPC IPAM's external
authority isn't in GovCloud.

548
00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:45.676
The Private Resolver is at FedRAMP
High in Azure Government and forecast,

549
00:38:45.676 --> 00:38:48.188
not yet authorized, at IL4 and IL5.

550
00:38:48.188 --> 00:38:51.687
Azure Private DNS can't
be signed anywhere.

551
00:38:51.687 --> 00:38:58.280
And informed network routing isn't
available in GCC-Moderate, GCC High or DoD.

552
00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:03.618
Module 4: the migration sequence
and operating the control plane.

553
00:39:03.618 --> 00:39:07.751
Name resolution has to keep
working while its authority moves,

554
00:39:07.751 --> 00:39:11.090
so Chapter 10 makes the
migration a series of small,

555
00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:16.018
reversible cutovers, each proven from
every origin before the next starts.

556
00:39:16.018 --> 00:39:20.727
Chapter 11 then covers what the
plane must support once it exists:

557
00:39:20.727 --> 00:39:25.350
scoped autonomy for divisions, one
API and audit trail, the query,

558
00:39:25.350 --> 00:39:30.931
lease and change evidence that OMB M-26-14
and an authorizing official ask for,

559
00:39:30.931 --> 00:39:34.943
and a troubleshooting reference
for the hybrid period.

560
00:39:34.943 --> 00:39:37.773
Appendix A's record-type
catalog and Appendix

561
00:39:37.773 --> 00:39:40.970
B's requirements mapping
sit underneath both.

562
00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:46.353
This is the book's canonical
sequence applied to the move from

563
00:39:46.353 --> 00:39:51.895
AD-integrated DNS and DHCP, plus
independent cloud DNS, to one policy plane.

564
00:39:51.895 --> 00:39:58.259
Phase 1, inventory: export every zone,
SRV record, forwarder and DHCP scope,

565
00:39:58.259 --> 00:40:01.017
and document resolution paths,

566
00:40:01.017 --> 00:40:06.532
including redirect-dns targets
and the OCSP and CRL endpoints.

567
00:40:06.532 --> 00:40:11.420
Phase 2, coexistence: new resolvers
and their Microsoft DNS and

568
00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:16.308
DHCP synchronization, with
forwarding so both systems answer.

569
00:40:16.308 --> 00:40:23.258
Phase 3, migrate authority: zone by zone,
clients in waves, redirect-dns retargeted.

570
00:40:23.258 --> 00:40:29.169
Phase 4, cloud federation: private endpoint
resolution validated from every origin.

571
00:40:29.169 --> 00:40:35.750
Phase 5, decommission AD DNS and DHCP, only
after reconciliation and failover tests,

572
00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:42.520
preserving the SRV and underscore msdcs
records remaining domain controllers need.

573
00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:46.882
Phase 6, hardening: Protective
DNS, encrypted DNS,

574
00:40:46.882 --> 00:40:51.245
DNSSEC where required,
monitoring and evidence.

575
00:40:51.245 --> 00:40:53.930
Each phase ends with
a rollback point and a

576
00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:57.383
resolution-path consistency
test, not a calendar date.

577
00:40:57.383 --> 00:41:02.116
The operational playbook splits the work
into eleven steps for an Azure-heavy

578
00:41:02.116 --> 00:41:06.121
estate; its durations are planning
estimates, and the gates decide.

579
00:41:06.121 --> 00:41:10.438
Hardening has no step of its own:
logging starts at the first step,

580
00:41:10.438 --> 00:41:13.208
and evidence is filed as each step closes.

581
00:41:13.208 --> 00:41:15.895
Every move of authority runs one loop.

582
00:41:15.895 --> 00:41:18.869
Lower the TTL, to three
hundred seconds at least

583
00:41:18.869 --> 00:41:21.995
forty-eight hours ahead,
recording the originals.

584
00:41:21.995 --> 00:41:28.106
Export, transform and import with a dry run,
never migrating SOA or apex NS records,

585
00:41:28.106 --> 00:41:33.032
and leaving locator records where
domain controllers register.

586
00:41:33.032 --> 00:41:36.720
Validate from every origin,
forward and reverse.

587
00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:39.233
Switch authority one mechanism at a time.

588
00:41:39.233 --> 00:41:42.819
Hold and observe until no
client queries the old server.

589
00:41:42.819 --> 00:41:46.008
Then restore the TTL
and file the evidence.

590
00:41:46.008 --> 00:41:48.235
A failed check reverts the switch,

591
00:41:48.235 --> 00:41:51.860
and the old authority,
still intact, answers again.

592
00:41:52.610 --> 00:41:56.093
Phase 1 is read-only, so it
carries no rollback risk,

593
00:41:56.093 --> 00:41:58.897
and its omission can't be repaired later.

594
00:41:58.897 --> 00:42:03.889
The assessment collects every zone
with its type, replication scope,

595
00:42:03.889 --> 00:42:09.318
update setting and signing state; every
record with type, TTL and timestamp;

596
00:42:09.318 --> 00:42:14.397
zone health, because stale records
migrate as faithfully as good ones;

597
00:42:14.397 --> 00:42:20.002
the locator records resolved from every
server and site, the Phase 5 baseline;

598
00:42:20.002 --> 00:42:25.431
forwarders tested over DNS; DNSSEC state;
DHCP scopes, options and failover;

599
00:42:25.431 --> 00:42:28.933
resolution paths; and the
OCSP and CRL endpoints,

600
00:42:28.933 --> 00:42:32.436
committed as JSON to the
assessment repository.

601
00:42:32.436 --> 00:42:36.451
Import from the record-level
export: Microsoft documents

602
00:42:36.451 --> 00:42:41.472
Export-DNS-Server-Zone as a troubleshooting
export, not zone-file format.

603
00:42:41.472 --> 00:42:44.035
Each component gets a target and a method.

604
00:42:44.035 --> 00:42:47.610
Internal zones become authoritative
zones on the platform.

605
00:42:47.610 --> 00:42:51.985
External zones need an NS change at
the registrar, so they're high risk.

606
00:42:51.985 --> 00:42:54.847
Stubs and forwarders
become forwarding rules.

607
00:42:54.847 --> 00:42:58.736
GlobalNames becomes CNAMEs
plus a suffix search list.

608
00:42:58.736 --> 00:43:02.658
Locator records stay where
domain controllers can register;

609
00:43:02.658 --> 00:43:06.581
there's no Azure-native equivalent,
because Azure Private DNS

610
00:43:06.581 --> 00:43:10.735
ignores client registrations and
imported SRV records go stale.

611
00:43:10.735 --> 00:43:14.523
Dynamic registration
becomes DHCP-driven updates.

612
00:43:14.523 --> 00:43:16.623
Reverse zones align to IPAM.

613
00:43:16.623 --> 00:43:21.215
Signed zones move in Phase 3 by their
own sequence, keeping the old signing

614
00:43:21.215 --> 00:43:25.884
until the new chain validates, and that
has to happen before the Phase 5 gate.

615
00:43:25.884 --> 00:43:29.097
Phase 2 adds resolvers
without moving authority.

616
00:43:29.097 --> 00:43:32.610
Synchronize read-only first,
and forward both ways.

617
00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:38.222
Give every namespace exactly one
authoritative path, or queries loop, and open

618
00:43:38.222 --> 00:43:43.747
UDP and TCP 53 from the resolver subnets
through the network security groups.

619
00:43:43.747 --> 00:43:49.864
Two errors cause most failures: an on-premises
forwarder pointed at 168.63.129.16,

620
00:43:49.864 --> 00:43:54.868
reachable only inside a virtual
network, and Windows forwarding

621
00:43:54.868 --> 00:44:00.680
timeouts of three seconds or less,
where Microsoft wants more than four.

622
00:44:01.430 --> 00:44:06.487
Phase 3 is where zones, forwarders,
DHCP scopes and clients actually move,

623
00:44:06.487 --> 00:44:09.942
and each move is one pass
through the cutover loop.

624
00:44:09.942 --> 00:44:13.672
Records follow the loop, then
observe for at least thirty days,

625
00:44:13.672 --> 00:44:18.280
with the old copy left in place as the
rollback point until decommissioning.

626
00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:22.405
Signed zones move here too,
by Chapter 06's sequence.

627
00:44:22.405 --> 00:44:25.093
Three patterns cover almost every zone.

628
00:44:25.093 --> 00:44:29.295
Split horizon, a private answer
inside and a public one outside,

629
00:44:29.295 --> 00:44:32.204
rolls back by withdrawing
the private view.

630
00:44:32.204 --> 00:44:36.153
Subdomain delegation, a child
moving while the parent stays,

631
00:44:36.153 --> 00:44:38.680
rolls back by removing the delegation.

632
00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:43.481
Progressive migration with conditional
forwarding moves whole zones one at a

633
00:44:43.481 --> 00:44:48.355
time and rolls back by restoring the
archived export and repointing forwarders.

634
00:44:48.355 --> 00:44:52.741
Check record types the target
handles differently before importing;

635
00:44:52.741 --> 00:44:54.480
Appendix A is the reference.

636
00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:59.368
SRV records are the most security-consequential
type in Active Directory

637
00:44:59.368 --> 00:45:03.705
and the most underestimated: nothing
moves them when AD is retired.

638
00:45:03.705 --> 00:45:07.702
PTR records are the most
neglected in cloud private space,

639
00:45:07.702 --> 00:45:10.867
so IPAM should create
the A and PTR together.

640
00:45:10.867 --> 00:45:15.367
A CNAME pointing at a deprovisioned
resource invites subdomain takeover.

641
00:45:15.367 --> 00:45:20.392
ALIAS and ANAME are provider-specific,
absent from the IANA registry.

642
00:45:20.392 --> 00:45:24.368
Re-test SPF, DKIM and
DMARC after the move.

643
00:45:24.368 --> 00:45:30.092
And HTTPS records missing from private zones
make clients behave differently inside.

644
00:45:30.092 --> 00:45:33.830
DHCP moves scope by
scope, with its options.

645
00:45:33.830 --> 00:45:37.028
Option 6 moves clients onto
the governed resolvers,

646
00:45:37.028 --> 00:45:39.705
so set it per scope and
check it after import.

647
00:45:39.705 --> 00:45:43.716
Keep failover through the move,
hot standby or load balance,

648
00:45:43.716 --> 00:45:46.418
and re-establish dynamic registration.

649
00:45:46.418 --> 00:45:51.592
Clients move last, in waves: pilot,
a department, then everyone.

650
00:45:51.592 --> 00:45:55.506
VMs keep old settings until
restart or lease renewal.

651
00:45:55.506 --> 00:45:59.598
And removing a VNet link from an
Azure Private DNS zone deletes

652
00:45:59.598 --> 00:46:04.005
its autoregistered records, which
Microsoft says can't be restored.

653
00:46:04.005 --> 00:46:06.050
Plan that rollback first.

654
00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:10.991
Phase 4 brings each cloud's DNS
and address data under the plane,

655
00:46:10.991 --> 00:46:14.456
with private endpoint zones
published once and the

656
00:46:14.456 --> 00:46:18.163
per-cloud forwarding rules
held in one place, Decision 3.

657
00:46:18.163 --> 00:46:21.114
Its contribution is the validation matrix:

658
00:46:21.114 --> 00:46:25.050
every class of name resolves
correctly from every origin.

659
00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:29.419
On-premises names resolve natively
on premises, through the outbound

660
00:46:29.419 --> 00:46:33.562
rule from each cloud, and through
the agency resolver from a branch.

661
00:46:33.562 --> 00:46:37.812
Cloud names resolve through the
inbound endpoint from on premises.

662
00:46:37.812 --> 00:46:41.637
Private endpoint names return the
private address from all three.

663
00:46:41.637 --> 00:46:46.613
Locator records resolve from each cloud
through the rule to the domain controllers.

664
00:46:46.613 --> 00:46:50.887
And OCSP and CRL endpoints
resolve everywhere.

665
00:46:50.887 --> 00:46:54.675
A private endpoint name returning
a public address from any

666
00:46:54.675 --> 00:46:58.613
origin is a failed test, even if
the application appears to work.

667
00:46:58.613 --> 00:47:02.901
Phase 5 is the one step whose mistakes
can't be rolled back cheaply,

668
00:47:02.901 --> 00:47:04.649
so it's gated, not scheduled.

669
00:47:04.649 --> 00:47:06.487
All five gates must pass.

670
00:47:06.487 --> 00:47:11.841
The inventory reconciles in both directions:
nothing missing, nothing unexplained,

671
00:47:11.841 --> 00:47:16.812
and every signed zone validating on its
new chain with the old signing stopped.

672
00:47:16.812 --> 00:47:20.813
Every critical locator record
resolves from every VNet and site,

673
00:47:20.813 --> 00:47:25.925
checked every fifteen minutes, at one
hundred percent for thirty consecutive days.

674
00:47:25.925 --> 00:47:31.237
Ninety days of parallel operation with
no critical alerts or unresolved drift.

675
00:47:31.237 --> 00:47:32.487
Failover tested.

676
00:47:32.487 --> 00:47:37.325
And a final query-log review shows
nothing still querying the legacy servers.

677
00:47:37.325 --> 00:47:43.074
Then decommission in order: remove
legacy DNS addresses from DHCP options,

678
00:47:43.074 --> 00:47:47.524
Group Policy and VNets; remove
forwarders for moved zones;

679
00:47:47.524 --> 00:47:52.160
archive the final exports;
de-authorize the old DHCP servers;

680
00:47:52.160 --> 00:47:55.962
only then remove the roles,
with a seven-day burn-in.

681
00:47:55.962 --> 00:47:59.905
The burn-in rollback reinstalls
the role from the archived exports,

682
00:47:59.905 --> 00:48:03.213
which is why they're archived
before anything is removed.

683
00:48:03.213 --> 00:48:06.369
Losing locator records
early causes forest-wide

684
00:48:06.369 --> 00:48:09.430
authentication and domain-join failures.

685
00:48:10.180 --> 00:48:14.095
A single policy plane only works if
divisions can still operate their

686
00:48:14.095 --> 00:48:17.943
networks through it, because a division
that can't will work around it.

687
00:48:17.943 --> 00:48:21.341
Corporate administrators
see the whole estate;

688
00:48:21.341 --> 00:48:26.014
each division gets a scope over its
zones, IP spaces and DHCP scopes,

689
00:48:26.014 --> 00:48:30.942
with manager, operator and auditor
roles and no visibility outside it.

690
00:48:30.942 --> 00:48:35.049
Infoblox access views are the book's
illustration, and the book notes

691
00:48:35.049 --> 00:48:39.517
they currently scope only IPAM roles,
so confirm what a release supports.

692
00:48:39.517 --> 00:48:43.392
Whatever stays on Windows or
Azure needs the same discipline.

693
00:48:43.392 --> 00:48:49.593
DnsAdmins grants full rights on DNS data,
and Microsoft lists it as privileged.

694
00:48:49.593 --> 00:48:53.608
Azure's DNS Zone Contributor
covers public zones only;

695
00:48:53.608 --> 00:48:57.892
Private DNS Zone Contributor
can be scoped to a record set.

696
00:48:57.892 --> 00:49:01.137
A role without delete
doesn't protect a record set,

697
00:49:01.137 --> 00:49:05.568
because modify can empty it, so add
resource locks on critical zones.

698
00:49:05.568 --> 00:49:11.774
Every object should sit behind one documented
API, so infrastructure as code, ITSM,

699
00:49:11.774 --> 00:49:17.341
ACME DNS-01 automation and pipelines
share the same roles and audit trail,

700
00:49:17.341 --> 00:49:21.630
with ACME accounts limited to
TXT records in their zones.

701
00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:22.593
Telemetry.

702
00:49:22.593 --> 00:49:32.456
M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31, whose tables
named DNS and DHCP logs, and names neither.

703
00:49:32.456 --> 00:49:36.827
Its baseline needs source and
destination network address information,

704
00:49:36.827 --> 00:49:39.980
searchable for six months
and retrievable for a year.

705
00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:44.134
The book's reading is that query
logs, which host asked for which name,

706
00:49:44.134 --> 00:49:47.992
and lease history, which device
held which address, supply that.

707
00:49:47.992 --> 00:49:52.678
Sources include the DDI resolvers,
the Windows DNS analytic log,

708
00:49:52.678 --> 00:49:57.275
about five percent cost at a
hundred thousand queries a second,

709
00:49:57.275 --> 00:50:02.940
Azure resolver policy logs, the DHCP
audit log, and the Azure activity log.

710
00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:07.402
Last, keeping it honest during
and after the hybrid period.

711
00:50:07.402 --> 00:50:11.428
Chapter 11's troubleshooting
reference covers the common failures.

712
00:50:11.428 --> 00:50:17.618
A private endpoint returning its public
address usually means the privatelink zone

713
00:50:17.618 --> 00:50:22.587
isn't linked to the querying network,
a forwarder targets 168.63.129.16,

714
00:50:22.587 --> 00:50:25.552
or a Windows forwarder times out too soon.

715
00:50:25.552 --> 00:50:29.960
Domain join failing from a cloud VM
means the locator records aren't

716
00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:35.153
resolvable from that network, so prove
SRV forwarding before any cloud VM joins.

717
00:50:35.153 --> 00:50:38.677
Queries that never resolve
are usually a forwarding loop.

718
00:50:38.677 --> 00:50:42.503
Old answers after a move are
cached within the old TTL.

719
00:50:42.503 --> 00:50:47.127
And DNSSEC failing after a move means
the parent's DS record wasn't updated.

720
00:50:47.127 --> 00:50:48.514
Watch continuously.

721
00:50:48.514 --> 00:50:51.897
Resolve the locator records
every fifteen minutes

722
00:50:51.897 --> 00:50:54.965
from every site and VNet,
site records included.

723
00:50:54.965 --> 00:50:59.670
Detect drift by normalizing both
sides to name, type and data,

724
00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:05.127
excluding SOA and NS, and commit each
report so the history is evidence.

725
00:51:05.127 --> 00:51:08.315
Report stale-record and
dangling-CNAME rates monthly.

726
00:51:08.315 --> 00:51:11.691
The Private Resolver has no
forwarding-failure metric,

727
00:51:11.691 --> 00:51:15.067
so alert on inbound queries
at zero in business hours or

728
00:51:15.067 --> 00:51:18.590
spiking over two hundred percent,
and use synthetic tests.

729
00:51:18.590 --> 00:51:24.342
The evidence checklist opens with the
five decisions, each checked live,

730
00:51:24.342 --> 00:51:30.191
then Protective DNS and encrypted DNS
evidence, IPAM inventory for CM-8,

731
00:51:30.191 --> 00:51:33.834
logs at M-26-14 retention,
rogue DHCP and router

732
00:51:33.834 --> 00:51:38.052
advertisement detection,
and reviewed delegations.

733
00:51:38.052 --> 00:51:41.427
Appendix B maps each
requirement to its evidence.

734
00:51:41.427 --> 00:51:46.782
So, before the next SD-WAN or DDI change:
audit every branch's redirect-dns target,

735
00:51:46.782 --> 00:51:50.406
verify the chain order in
the running configuration,

736
00:51:50.406 --> 00:51:55.020
complete the AD DNS inventory before
touching a domain controller,

737
00:51:55.020 --> 00:52:00.127
confirm the sovereign-cloud limits,
and take open questions to the vendors.

738
00:52:00.127 --> 00:52:02.544
Chapter 09 assigns who does each,

739
00:52:02.544 --> 00:52:07.377
and Chapter 12 measures success
as resolution-path consistency.

740
00:52:07.377 --> 00:52:08.200
Thank you.
